**user**:
> I am running a literature review, and I would like to include instructions that ask the reviewers of papers to match up identified papers with how they would fit into an outline of the output. Here the outline:
> ```
> Agriculture Subsection Outline (Ariel Ortiz-Bobea)
> NCA5 Chapter 19 treated agriculture briefly: a few rows in Table 19.1, a paragraph on CO2 fertilization, and passing mentions of food prices and supply chains. Since then, the economics literature has advanced on (1) quantifying damages net of adaptation, (2) documenting the limits and costs of adaptation, (3) measuring indirect and cascading effects, and (4) identifying policy barriers: particularly in crop insurance and water. This subsection synthesizes those advances.
> 1.	The scale and scope of agricultural climate damages
> Since NCA5, new evidence confirms that climate change is already reducing U.S. and global agricultural productivity, with damages larger and more pervasive than previously estimated.
> Key papers:
> •	Ortiz-Bobea, Chambers, He & Lobell (2025, PNAS): climate change requires $2.2-3.8B/year in additional R&D to offset U.S. agricultural TFP decline
> •	Ortiz-Bobea et al. (2021, Nature Climate Change):  anthropogenic climate change has reduced global agricultural TFP by ~21% since 1961 [NCA5 baseline]
> •	Lobell & Di Tommaso (2025, PNAS):  50-year agroclimatic trends show North America has been "mostly spared" relative to other regions, but models project this grace period will end
> •	American Farm Bureau Federation Market Intel (2023-2024, using USDA RMA data): $20-21B/year in U.S. crop losses from extreme weather
> Key point: Establish that agricultural damages are already occurring and that the U.S. has been relatively buffered so far relative to other parts of the world (but this is changing).
> 2.	Direct crop yield impacts: What we now know
> A growing body of work has refined estimates of how temperature extremes affect crop yields, validated these methods across data sources and geographies, and projected substantial U.S. production declines.
> Key papers:
> •	Hultgren et al. (2025, Nature): first global estimate of crop yield losses explicitly accounting for adaptation: ~24% decline under high emissions by 2100
> •	Hogan & Schlenker (2024, Nature Communications): validates that global gridded weather data (ERA5-Land) captures the nonlinear temperature-yield relationships first documented by Schlenker & Roberts (2009)
> •	Fei, Jagermeyr, McCarl et al. (2023, Anthropocene): U.S.-specific projections: corn yields in central states decline 14.5%, soybeans 7.1%; Kansas/Nebraska/Oklahoma corn up to -25.4%
> Key point: The damage estimates have gotten more precise, more geographically resolved, and now account for farmer adaptation (which previous estimates assumed away or treated as perfect).
> 3.	Adaptation is real but insufficient
> A central advance since NCA5 is the accumulation of evidence that farmers do adapt to climate change, but that adaptation offsets only a fraction of damages: roughly one-quarter to one-third.
> Key papers:
> •	Hultgren et al. (2025, Nature): adaptation offsets ~1/3 of global crop yield losses
> •	Burke et al. (2024, NBER WP): across 21 outcomes over 50 years, "limited systematic evidence of adaptation"
> •	Bareille & Chakir (2024, AJAE): structural model: within-season input adjustments offset 25-67% of warming damages
> •	Gammans, Merel & Ortiz-Bobea (2025, AJAE): double cropping as U.S. adaptation: net revenue gain only 1.8% under 3C warming
> •	Burke & Emerick (2016, AEJ:EP): foundational long-differences result showing near-zero long-run adaptation in U.S. agriculture [the benchmark post-NCA5 work responds to]
> •	Moscona & Sastry (2023, QJE): directed innovation in seed varieties responds to heat exposure, but magnitude is insufficient to offset damages
> Key point: NCA5 noted adaptation uncertainty with "medium confidence." The literature has since converged: adaptation is happening but falls far short of what's needed.
> 4.	Barriers to adaptation: Insurance, information, and behavior
> Federal crop insurance: the largest U.S. agricultural safety net program: may be inadvertently hindering climate adaptation by reducing farmers' incentives to adjust to rising temperatures.
> Key papers:
> •	Annan & Schlenker (2015, AER P&P): insured crops are more sensitive to extreme heat than uninsured crops [a bit old, but first study to try to tackle this]
> •	Regmi (2023, JAAEA): farm-level evidence: insured dryland corn is 38% more heat-sensitive than uninsured
> •	Deryugina & Konar (2017, AWR): crop insurance causally increases irrigation water withdrawals
> •	Chemeris, Liu & Ker (2022, Food Policy): insurance subsidies inhibit adoption of climate-resilient crop varieties
> •	Annan & Schlenker (2024/2025, NBER volume): projects rising fiscal cost of crop insurance under climate change
> •	Aglasan et al. (2024, AJAE): cover crop adoption reduces crop insurance losses from extreme weather: a potential reform pathway
> •	Patel (2024, WP): farmer misperceptions about environmental change impede adaptation; information corrections alter behavior
> Key point: NCA5 mentioned crop insurance costs rising; the new literature shows insurance is actively working against adaptation through moral hazard. This is a first-order policy finding.
> 5.	Water and irrigation under climate change
> Climate change is accelerating demand for irrigation at the same time that major U.S. aquifers are being depleted, and new evidence quantifies both the economic costs of depletion and the institutional barriers to efficient water reallocation.
> Key papers:
> •	Perez-Quesada, Hendricks & Steward (2024, JAERE): economic cost of Ogallala depletion: $126.7M/year by 2050, $266M/year by 2100
> •	Cameron-Harp & Hendricks (2025, JAERE): efficient irrigation technology does conserve water (no Jevons paradox/rebound)
> •	Earnhart & Hendricks (2023, AJAE): farmers adapt to water restrictions along intensive then extensive margins
> •	Bruno & Jessoe (2024, Nature Climate Change): water markets can serve as low-cost climate adaptation, but institutional design is critical
> •	Hagerty (2024, WP): surface water scarcity in California: crop substitution occurs but does not offset lost production
> •	Libecap (2024, ed., UChicago Press): 19th-century water rights institutions raise transaction costs, impeding flexible climate response
> •	Zhao, Hendricks & Li (2024, ARRE): groundwater institutions need polycentric governance to handle climate-driven demand increases
> Key point: NCA5 did not discuss water-agriculture-climate economics. This is a gap now being filled, with a strong cluster of recent work on the High Plains Aquifer.
> 6.	Indirect and cascading effects through trade and supply chains
> Agricultural climate damages propagate well beyond the farm gate: through trade policy responses, supply chains, and food markets: and these indirect effects may be larger than the direct crop losses themselves.
> Key papers:
> •	Hsiao, Moscona & Sastry (2026, Econometrica): governments respond to domestic heat shocks with consumer-assisting trade policies, but these are globally regressive: they reduce domestic consumer losses 21% while increasing foreign consumer losses 32%
> •	Nath (2025, JPE): trade barriers trap poor countries in agriculture as climate makes farming less productive; trade liberalization reduces damages ~70%
> •	Zappala (2024, JMP): extreme heat in agriculture propagates to downstream sectors via supply chain linkages; effects are persistent
> •	Kotz et al. (2024, Comms Earth & Environ): warming projected to increase food price inflation 1.4-1.8 pp/year in North America by 2035
> Key point: NCA5 mentioned supply chain risks briefly. The new literature shows that the policy and trade responses to agricultural climate shocks are themselves a major source of economic damage, particularly for vulnerable populations.
> 7.	Livestock, labor, and understudied sectors
> Despite the large economic value of U.S. livestock production and the vulnerability of agricultural workers to heat stress, rigorous economic evidence on these topics remains remarkably thin.
> Key papers:
> •	Hutchins, Skidmore & Nolan (2025, Food Policy): first large-scale micro study of U.S. dairy heat stress: small herds lose 1.6%/year vs. 0.7% for large herds; damages expected to increase ~30% by 2050
> •	Hrozencik & Perez-Quesada (2025, Ag Economics): USDA's Livestock Forage Disaster Program provides financial relief but does not change rancher adaptation behavior
> •	EDF & La Isla Network (2023): crop workers are 20x more likely to die from heat than private industry workers; Gubernot et al. (2015, AJIM) reports 35x vs. all industries; piece-rate pay exacerbates risk
> •	Graff Zivin & Neidell (2014, JOLE): high temperatures reduce hours worked in climate-exposed industries including agriculture [first study to look at heat effects on labor supply]
> 
> Gaps to highlight:
> •	No rigorous economics paper on U.S. beef cattle and climate
> •	No U.S.-specific farmworker heat-productivity study using economic methods
> •	Livestock production value exceeds crop value, yet the economics literature on livestock-climate is a fraction of the crop literature
> Key point: Flag these as critical gaps. The NCA6 review should note that the economics profession has not kept up with the policy need in these sectors.
> 8.	Distributional impacts and food security
> Climate-driven agricultural damages are distributed unevenly across U.S. regions, farm sizes, and racial groups, and may exacerbate existing food insecurity disparities: but the economics evidence on these pathways remains sparse.
> Key papers:
> •	Hultgren et al. (2025, Nature): wealthiest agricultural regions lose 41% of yields, lowest-income regions 28%: damages are regressive within agriculture
> •	Hutchins et al. (2025, Food Policy): small dairy farms disproportionately vulnerable to heat stress
> •	USDA/CBPP (2024-2025): baseline food insecurity rates: 24.4% Black, 20.2% Hispanic, 30.9% American Indian/Alaska Native households vs. 10.1% non-Hispanic white
> •	NBER Conference on Distributional Impacts of Climate Change in Agriculture (Fall 2023): dedicated conference identified this as a priority but few published results yet
> Gaps to highlight:
> •	Racial/ethnic disparities in agricultural climate impacts are documented in policy reports but not in economics journals
> •	Rural community economic impacts of agricultural decline are essentially unstudied
> Key point: NCA5 emphasized inequality (Figure 19.2) but had thin agriculture-specific evidence. The gap persists: this should be flagged as a priority for future research.
> 9.	Methodological advances and how to read the evidence
> New methodological work clarifies a fundamental tension in the climate damages literature: no single empirical approach can simultaneously be model-free, estimate long-run climate effects, and be causally identified.
> Key papers:
> •	Lemoine, Hausman & Shrader (2025, NBER WP): identifies the "trilemma": researchers must choose which of three desirable properties to sacrifice
> •	Merel, Paroissien & Gammans (2024, JEEM): derives conditions under which short-run weather estimates can identify long-run climate effects; U.S. ag GDP declines 6-10% under 2C
> •	Gouel (2022, NBER WP): supply-side damage estimates can have the opposite sign from equilibrium welfare measures
> •	Hogan & Schlenker (2024, Handbook): comprehensive survey of empirical approaches to climate impact quantification
> •	Bareille & Chakir (2023, JEEM): repeat-Ricardian approach shows standard cross-sectional estimates understate damages by 56-96%
> Key point: Give readers a framework for interpreting the different damage numbers cited throughout the section. The trilemma paper explains why studies sometimes disagree.
> 10.	What we still don't know
> Despite significant progress since NCA5, several first-order questions about the economic impacts of climate change on U.S. agriculture remain unanswered.
> Key gaps to list:
> 1.	How will livestock production (beef, dairy, poultry) respond to warming, and what are the welfare implications?
> 2.	How does heat affect U.S. farmworker productivity and labor supply, and how do labor markets adjust?
> 3.	How do climate-driven crop losses translate to U.S. retail food prices and food security?
> 4.	What insurance and water policy reforms would promote rather than hinder adaptation?
> 5.	What are the limits to adaptation: when does marginal adaptation cost exceed marginal damage avoided?
> 6.	How do climate damages interact across sectors (e.g., heat stress reduces both crop yields and the feed supply for livestock, while increasing irrigation demand from a depleting aquifer)?
> 7.	When do climatic shock leads to persistent climatic effects?
> Key papers for framing:
> •	Carleton, Duflo, Jack & Zappala (2025, NBER WP / Handbook): comprehensive survey finds adaptation literature offers limited guidance for policy design
> •	Du Puy & Shrader (2024, WP): first direct estimates of the marginal cost of adapting to temperature changes
> •	Ortiz-Bobea, Chambers, He & Lobell (2025, PNAS): R&D investment is one lever, but the required scale ($2.2-3.8B/year) signals the magnitude of the challenge
> ```
> 
